import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageMeta from '@/components/PageMeta.astro';
import Diagram from '@/components/Diagram.astro';
---

<Layout title="Contexts and common boundary" description="How SPDTF assigns semantic homes, preserves local meaning, defines a small common boundary and treats the Property Pack as a cross-context profile.">
  <PageMeta category="How we model SPDTF" variant="brand" updated="Context ownership · common boundary · Property Pack profile" />
  <h1>Bounded contexts and the common boundary</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A bounded context is the boundary within which a model and its language have a
    particular meaning. SPDTF keeps context ownership visible and makes cross-context
    agreements explicit instead of forcing one universal definition too early.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contexts">Property contexts and scheme context</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>Initial property-domain contexts and the separate scheme-design lens</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Context</th><th scope="col">Illustrative scope</th><th scope="col">Type</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Finance and Banking</th><td>Mortgage journey, lending decisions, parties, evidence, risk and finance-data exchange.</td><td>Property-domain context</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Conveyancing</th><td>Instruction, legal transfer, enquiries, searches, exchange and completion.</td><td>Property-domain context</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Estate Agency</th><td>Marketing, listings, material information, viewings, offers and seller information.</td><td>Property-domain context</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Surveying and Valuation</th><td>Inspection, condition, valuation, professional evidence and property risk.</td><td>Property-domain context</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Property Data Services</th><td>Registries, searches, authoritative sources, provenance, currency and reuse.</td><td>Property-domain context</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Property Technology</th><td>Platforms, workflow, integration, APIs, implementation and operational feedback.</td><td>Property-domain context</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">DBT Smart Data</th><td>Participants, roles, trust, consent, authorisation, accreditation, liability and cross-sector alignment.</td><td>OPDA-internal scheme-design context; not a government group or property-domain context</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="homes">One semantic home, many consumers</h2>
  <p>
    Each OPDA-defined SPDTF resource has one semantic home: the context accountable for
    its definition and substantive change. An externally governed resource keeps its
    external home; OPDA records the contexts that consume it. Consumption does not
    transfer ownership.
  </p>
  <Diagram size="large" caption="Representative contexts retain local meaning. Interoperability owns only the small common boundary and qualified mappings. The Property Pack selects across contexts as a delivery profile; it does not become another universal domain model.">
flowchart LR
  accTitle: Context-owned models common boundary and Property Pack profile
  accDescr: Four representative property contexts contribute requirements to an interoperability boundary; the Property Pack profile selects reviewed meanings across them while the DBT scheme context remains separate.
  Agency["Estate agency"]:::user
  Conveyancing["Conveyancing"]:::security
  Finance["Finance and banking"]:::data
  Surveying["Surveying and valuation"]:::infra
  Interop["Interoperability"]:::process
  Common["Small common boundary"]:::service
  Pack["Property Pack profile"]:::external
  Scheme["DBT scheme-design context"]:::xsection
  Agency --> Interop
  Conveyancing --> Interop
  Finance --> Interop
  Surveying --> Interop
  Interop --> Common
  Agency --> Pack
  Conveyancing --> Pack
  Common --> Pack
  Scheme -.-> Interop
  </Diagram>

  <h2 id="semantic-home">Semantic home decision</h2>
  <p>
    The home is chosen from the business meaning, not from whichever spreadsheet first
    supplied a field. Reuse an external resource when its governance and meaning fit;
    otherwise identify the context that owns the distinction. Put something in the
    common boundary only when several contexts require one stable exchange agreement.
  </p>
  <Diagram size="large" caption="The decision starts with a reviewed meaning. Suitable governed external terms are reused; otherwise a domain context owns the term. Only a genuine multi-context exchange agreement enters the common boundary.">
flowchart TB
  accTitle: How an SPDTF resource receives a semantic home
  accDescr: A reviewed meaning is checked against governed external terms, then assigned to a domain owner; shared exchange needs may contribute a bounded common term, otherwise contexts use a qualified mapping.
  Meaning["Reviewed business meaning"]:::user
  External["Suitable governed external term?"]:::external
  Reuse["Reuse with exact profile"]:::success
  Owner["Assign one domain owner"]:::process
  Shared["Stable multi-context agreement needed?"]:::security
  Common["Contribute to common boundary"]:::service
  Mapping["Keep local terms and qualify mapping"]:::data
  Meaning --> External
  External -->|yes| Reuse
  External -->|no| Owner
  Owner --> Shared
  Shared -->|yes| Common
  Shared -->|no| Mapping
  </Diagram>

  <h2 id="same-label">Same label does not guarantee same meaning</h2>
  <p>
    “Property”, “client”, “instruction” or “completion” may have legitimate local senses.
    If two contexts mean the same identifiable thing, reuse one resource. If they mean
    different things, mint context-owned resources and record a justified mapping where
    useful. Do not use <code>owl:sameAs</code> as a convenience; it asserts identity more
    strongly than most cross-context comparisons support. The
    <a href="/spdtf/ontologies/evidence-and-mappings#cross-context-mappings">Category 8 mapping method</a>
    separates the architectural context map from any term-level SKOS assertion and its provenance record.
  </p>

  <h2 id="common">The small common boundary</h2>
  <p>
    ADR-0067 assigns Interoperability responsibility as a peer of the domain groups; this
    wording does not claim that the group is already convened. Its remit is the context
    map, qualified cross-context mappings, shared identifier/provenance/version conventions
    and only the small set of meanings that genuinely require a common exchange contract.
    A boundary agreement is not permission to redesign a domain model centrally. Mapping
    candidates remain advisory until affected semantic owners and Interoperability review them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="property-pack">The Property Pack is a cross-context profile</h2>
  <p>
    A Property Pack is a standardised bundle of property information for a transaction.
    Its ontology is a profile spanning several semantic contexts: it selects the meanings,
    values and constraints required for that delivery. It is not itself a bounded context,
    and its 451 source items do not imply 451 ontology properties.
  </p>
  <p>
    The current <a href="/spdtf/property-pack">Property Pack ontology candidate</a>
    proposes semantic homes by machine. The Technical Working Group will determine the
    accelerated component; later domain review may propose governed changes. That sequence
    must remain visible in every interpretation of the candidate. The Technical Working
    Group is not the Property Technology bounded-context group listed above; its exact
    delegated remit and decision record belong to Property Pack governance.
  </p>
</Layout>
